        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Ectopic tubal pregnancy, surgically treated</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 85-year-old female patient, admitted on 03/12/1981 with acute lower abdominal pain and hemodynamic instability. On admission, a tubal pregnancy was suspected based on clinical presentation. Diagnostic workup including pelvic ultrasonography and serum beta-hCG confirmed a right-sided tubal ectopic pregnancy. Given the patient's hemodynamic compromise and advanced rupture risk, we proceeded with emergency surgical intervention. A laparoscopic salpingectomy with removal of the ectopic pregnancy was performed without complications. Postoperative recovery was uneventful. Vital signs were closely monitored throughout the stay. Intravenous fluid replacement and analgesic medication were administered perioperatively. The patient was mobilized successfully and discharged home on 09/12/1981 in stable condition. We recommend close follow-up in your practice. Please do not hesitate to contact us should any questions arise.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
